{"authored" [{"question" "q-275781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+z=-1 \\\\ x-y+3z=2 \\\\ -2x+y-z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, and the inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe solution of the system of equations is \n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}, \n$\\,z=$\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "125b930adbc81933b58b38886760ab94b8bf8fd24cd18ba2b9bce5c1c83a100e" "basis" 1065 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is \n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=0,\\,z=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995994/state.json" "file_sha256" "125b930adbc81933b58b38886760ab94b8bf8fd24cd18ba2b9bce5c1c